A thread that becomes ready joins a queue of the core that settles it rather than of the core it runs on,
so a signal from another core puts the thread where its units are not; each core's queues hold its own threads.

Caught-by: fuzz-smp2
Report: a core's queue holds a thread of another core

diff --git a/kernel/sched.c b/kernel/sched.c
--- a/kernel/sched.c
+++ b/kernel/sched.c
@@ -247,7 +247,7 @@ static void thread_settle(struct thread *t)
             ring_remove(queue_head(was, t->queue), t);
         }
         if (queue != QUEUE_NONE) {
-            ring_push(queue_head(core, queue), t);
+            ring_push(queue_head(core_self(), queue), t);
         }
         t->queue = queue;
     }
